<misterromdescription>
  <name>Grid Seeker: Project Storm Hammer (Ver 1.3O)</name>
  <setname>gseeker</setname>
  <year>1992</year>
  <manufacturer>Taito</manufacturer>
  <rbf>TaitoF3</rbf>
  <players>2</players>
  <joystick>8-way</joystick>
  <rotation>vertical</rotation>
  <region>World</region>

  <!-- ============================================================
       NEVER LOADED ON A BOARD. Written 2026-09-08.

       Taito F3, the same board Ray Force runs on, and like Ray Force
       a VERTICAL shooter (MAME ROT90), so Rotate CW or CCW in the
       OSD rather than None.

         - visarea f3_224b (224 lines from 32), one line down from
           Ray Force's f3_224a. Implemented already.
         - extend = 0 (eight 32x32 playfields), as Puzzle Bobble 2
         - sprite lag 1 in MAME's table; the engine is structurally
           lag 2, as for every other game here bar Ray Force

       THREE THINGS THIS SET DOES THAT NO EARLIER MRA HERE HAD TO:

       1. THE SPRITE ROMs CARRY A FACTORY PATCH. MAME's comment says
          it plainly: "Taito manufactured mask roms 3 + 4 wrong, and
          later added 15 + 16 as a patch". So d40_03/04 are loaded
          and then d40_15/16 are loaded OVER the bottom 1 MB of them.
          An MRA concatenates and cannot overwrite, so the patch is
          applied here by hand: the bottom megabyte comes from the
          patch ROMs, the top from the upper halves of the originals
          (offset 0x80000). The bottom halves of d40_03/04 and the
          ROM_CONTINUE that placed them are dead data in MAME too;
          nothing reads them once the patch has landed.

       2. THE SOUND ROM IS HALF SIZE, AND IS MIRRORED HERE. MAME's
          audiocpu region is 0x140000 against Ray Force's 0x180000,
          so taito_en computes two bank entries where Ray Force has
          four, and its reset mapping is set_entry(i % max) = 0,1,0.
          This core maps the sound 68000's C00000-C7FFFF LINEARLY to
          SDRAM 0x200000 and does not bank, which is correct only
          while the map is linear. Streaming the 256 KB TWICE makes
          the third window read the first bank again, which is
          exactly what "i % 2" does on the real board. Padding with
          zeros instead would put silence where the game expects
          code.

       3. THE ENSONIQ BANKS ARE SPARSE. d40_01 fills banks 0 and 1,
          bank 2 is empty, and d40_02 sits in bank 3 (MAME offset
          0x600000). The 1 MB hole is padded so bank 3 lands on a
          bank boundary. The region is 8 MB, so the otisbank mask is
          3, the same as Ray Force.

       The universal F3 SDRAM map, and where this set pads:

         0x0000000  maincpu    2 MB   4x 256 KB + 1 MB pad
         0x0200000  audiocpu 512 KB   256 KB, streamed twice
         0x0280000  sprites    4 MB   2 MB patched + 2 MB pad
         0x0680000  sprites_hi 2 MB   ZEROS, no ROM in this set
         0x0880000  tilemap    4 MB   2 MB + 2 MB pad
         0x0C80000  tilemap_hi 2 MB   ZEROS, no ROM in this set
         0x0E80000  ensoniq    4 MB   2 MB + 1 MB hole + 1 MB
         0x1280000             = 18.5 MB total
       ============================================================ -->
  <rom index="0" zip="gseeker.zip" md5="None">

    <!-- maincpu: ROM_LOAD32_BYTE x4 -->
    <interleave output="32">
      <part name="d40_12.rom" crc="884055fb" map="0001"/>
      <part name="d40_11.rom" crc="85e701d2" map="0010"/>
      <part name="d40_10.rom" crc="1e659ac5" map="0100"/>
      <part name="d40_14.rom" crc="d9a76bd9" map="1000"/>
    </interleave>

    <!-- pad: maincpu 1 MB -> 2 MB -->
    <part repeat="1048576">00</part>

    <!-- audiocpu 68000: 256 KB, then the SAME 256 KB again. See note 2. -->
    <interleave output="16">
      <part name="d40_07.rom" crc="7e9b26c2" map="01"/>
      <part name="d40_08.rom" crc="9c926a28" map="10"/>
    </interleave>
    <interleave output="16">
      <part name="d40_07.rom" crc="7e9b26c2" map="01"/>
      <part name="d40_08.rom" crc="9c926a28" map="10"/>
    </interleave>

    <!-- sprites 0x000000-0x0FFFFF: the PATCH ROMs. See note 1. -->
    <interleave output="16">
      <part name="d40_15.rom" crc="50555125" map="01"/>
      <part name="d40_16.rom" crc="3f9bbe1e" map="10"/>
    </interleave>

    <!-- sprites 0x100000-0x1FFFFF: upper halves of the original masks -->
    <interleave output="16">
      <part name="d40_03.rom" crc="bcd70efc" offset="0x80000" length="0x80000" map="01"/>
      <part name="d40_04.rom" crc="cd2ac666" offset="0x00000" length="0x80000" map="10"/>
    </interleave>

    <!-- pad: sprites 2 MB -> 4 MB -->
    <part repeat="2097152">00</part>

    <!-- sprites_hi: none in this set (EMPTY_SPRITE_HIDATA) -->
    <part repeat="2097152">00</part>

    <!-- tilemap: ROM_LOAD32_WORD pair -->
    <interleave output="32">
      <part name="d40_05.rom" crc="be6eec8f" map="0021"/>
      <part name="d40_06.rom" crc="a822abe4" map="2100"/>
    </interleave>

    <!-- pad: tilemap 2 MB -> 4 MB -->
    <part repeat="2097152">00</part>

    <!-- tilemap_hi: none in this set (EMPTY_TILEMAP_HIDATA) -->
    <part repeat="2097152">00</part>

    <!-- ensoniq: banks 0 and 1 -->
    <part name="d40_01.rom" crc="ee312e95"/>

    <!-- ensoniq bank 2: empty in MAME, so a 1 MB hole -->
    <part repeat="1048576">00</part>

    <!-- ensoniq bank 3 (MAME offset 0x600000) -->
    <part name="d40_02.rom" crc="ed894fe1"/>
  </rom>

  <!-- Game config (Rayforce.sv "GAME CONFIG"):
         index 1 = 0xC5
           bit [1:0] = 1  visarea f3_224b (224 lines from line 32)
           bit [2]   = 1  asks for NON-extend (eight 32x32 playfields).
                          Inverted sense against MAME's "extend" naming,
                          because every MRA written before this bit went
                          live carries 0 there and all of those are
                          extend=1.
           bit [4:3] = 0  zone injector off, so it starts on level 1
         index 2 = 0x01
           game id = {index2[2:0], index1[5], index1[7:6]} = 11.
           No expectations are measured for this set yet, so it lands on
           the case default in Rayforce.sv and the ROM CHECKSUM / SDRAM
           BIST / WRITE HASH rows REPORT what they find rather than
           failing against another game's numbers. The self-test TITLE
           row clamps (rf_selftest.sv:329) until a build gives this game
           its own row. That is the page, not the game. -->
  <rom index="1">
    <part>C5</part>
  </rom>
  <rom index="2">
    <part>01</part>
  </rom>

  <!-- the 93C46 settings EEPROM: 64 words, saved to config/nvram/<mra>.nvm -->
  <nvram index="254" size="256"/>

  <buttons names="Shot,Bomb,-,-,-,-,Start,Coin,Service,Pause" default="A,B,R,L,Select,Start" count="2"/>
</misterromdescription>
